+The interpretation is conditional on the fixed SHAP background and the tested model configurations. The main grouped analysis used 100 training windows shared across models and seeds; sensitivity to alternative background selections was not evaluated and is not claimed. Coalition enumeration does not remove this uncertainty, and interventional masking can break dependencies among correlated predictors. Five seeds provide limited information about training variability, with only four degrees of freedom for the seed-based intervals. The less stable BU-LSTM-Attention ablations, the overlap between the random and upper-tail SHAP samples, and comparisons of rankings across different evaluation subsets limit broader conclusions. Attribution magnitudes and ranks should therefore be read as model- and background-dependent associations rather than causal effects. \revminor{Previous methodological research has shown that Shapley-value explanations can be misleading when dependencies among correlated features are not adequately accounted for \citep{aas2021explaining}. This reinforces the need to interpret the present attributions cautiously, without assuming that they uniquely identify the contribution of each meteorological predictor.}
 
 Finally, the present study focuses on one regional forecasting problem using ERA5 predictors over Dublin. The results should therefore not be interpreted as evidence that the observed model ranking will hold across other climates, spatial scales, or temporal resolutions. Applying the same evaluation protocol to other regions would help determine which findings are specific to the Dublin dataset and which are more general. In particular, the relative behaviour of recurrent models and XGBoost under upper-tail precipitation, together with the gap between overall and upper-tail probabilistic coverage, would be useful to examine across regions with different precipitation regimes.
 
